<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-elementary-logic">
<title>Elementary Logic</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Familiarity with mathematical statements and notation from
<xref ref="sec-mathematical-language-notation" />, sets from
<xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory" />, relations from <xref ref="sec-relations" />, and
functions from <xref ref="sec-functions" />.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>distinguish propositions from nonpropositions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify truth values;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret negation, conjunction, disjunction, implication, and biconditional statements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>construct and interpret truth tables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish converse, inverse, and contrapositive statements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret necessary and sufficient conditions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize tautologies, contradictions, and contingencies;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine logical equivalence;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with predicates and quantifiers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>negate quantified statements correctly;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret statements with multiple quantifiers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize common valid and invalid forms of argument;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect logical operations with set operations and proof methods.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-why-logic-matters">
<title>Why Logic Matters</title>
<p>Logic studies the structure of valid reasoning.</p>

<p>Mathematical definitions, theorems, proofs, algorithms, and formal arguments
all rely on logical structure.</p>

<p>For example, when a theorem states</p>

<p>
<md>P\Longrightarrow Q,</md>
</p>

<p>mathematics is asserting that whenever statement <m>P</m> is true, statement
<m>Q</m> must also be true.</p>

<p>Understanding such statements is essential before studying formal proofs.</p>

<p>Logic also provides a bridge between ordinary language and mathematical
reasoning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-propositions">
<title>Propositions</title>
<definition>
<title>Proposition</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>proposition</em> is a declarative statement that has exactly one truth
value: true or false.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><m>2+3=5</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>7&lt;4</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>every square is nonnegative;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>11</m> is even.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The first and third propositions are true.</p>

<p>The second and fourth are false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-nonpropositions">
<title>Statements That Are Not Propositions</title>
<p>Questions, commands, and expressions with unspecified variables are generally
not propositions.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><q>What is @@PROTECTED0@@?</q></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Solve the equation.</q></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>x+2=7</m></p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>are not propositions as written.</p>

<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>x+2=7</md>
</p>

<p>may become a proposition after a value of <m>x</m> is specified or after the
variable is quantified.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-truth-values">
<title>Truth Values</title>
<p>A proposition has one of two truth values:</p>

<p>
<md>\text{True}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\text{False}.</md>
</p>

<p>These are often abbreviated</p>

<p>
<md>T\qquad\text{and}\qquad F.</md>
</p>

<p>In formal logic and computer science, true and false may also be represented
by other conventions such as <m>1</m> and <m>0</m>, depending on context.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-variables">
<title>Logical Variables</title>
<p>Letters such as</p>

<p>
<md>P,\qquad Q,\qquad R</md>
</p>

<p>are commonly used to represent propositions.</p>

<p>For example, let</p>

<p>
<md>P:\text{ ``\(8\) is even''}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Q:\text{ ``\(8&gt;10\)''}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then <m>P</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-negation">
<title>Negation</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\neg</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>negation symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>not.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Negation</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>negation</em> of a proposition <m>P</m>, written</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P,</md>
</p>

<p>is true exactly when <m>P</m> is false.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>P:\text{ ``\(5&gt;2\)''},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P:\text{ ``\(5\) is not greater than \(2\)''}.</md>
</p>

<p>Since <m>P</m> is true, <m>\neg P</m> is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-negation-truth-table">
<title>Truth Table for Negation</title>
<p>A <em>truth table</em> lists all possible truth values of logical expressions.</p>

<p>For negation,</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c}
P &amp; \neg P\\ \hline
T &amp; F\\
F &amp; T
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>Negation reverses the truth value.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-conjunction">
<title>Conjunction</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\land</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>logical conjunction symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>and.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Conjunction</title>
<statement>
<p>The conjunction</p>

<p>
<md>P\land Q</md>
</p>

<p>is true exactly when both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are true.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example, the statement</p>

<p>
<md>5&gt;2\land 8&gt;3</md>
</p>

<p>is true.</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>5&gt;2\land 8&lt;3</md>
</p>

<p>is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-conjunction-truth-table">
<title>Truth Table for Conjunction</title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
P &amp; Q &amp; P\land Q\\ \hline
T &amp; T &amp; T\\
T &amp; F &amp; F\\
F &amp; T &amp; F\\
F &amp; F &amp; F
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>The conjunction is true only in the first row.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-disjunction">
<title>Disjunction</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\lor</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>logical disjunction symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>or.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Disjunction</title>
<statement>
<p>The disjunction</p>

<p>
<md>P\lor Q</md>
</p>

<p>is true when at least one of <m>P</m> or <m>Q</m> is true.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>In standard mathematical logic, <q>or</q> is inclusive.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>P\lor Q</m> is also true when both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are true.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-disjunction-truth-table">
<title>Truth Table for Disjunction</title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
P &amp; Q &amp; P\lor Q\\ \hline
T &amp; T &amp; T\\
T &amp; F &amp; T\\
F &amp; T &amp; T\\
F &amp; F &amp; F
\end{array}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-inclusive-exclusive-or">
<title>Inclusive versus Exclusive Or</title>
<p>Ordinary conversation sometimes uses <q>or</q> exclusively.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p><q>You may choose soup or salad.</q></p>

<p>may imply that exactly one should be chosen.</p>

<p>Mathematical logic normally uses inclusive or:</p>

<p>
<md>P\lor Q</md>
</p>

<p>means that one or both propositions are true.</p>

<p>When exactly one should be true, this is called <em>exclusive or</em>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-implication">
<title>Implication</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\to</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>implication arrow</em> in logic.</p>

<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>if @@PROTECTED0@@, then @@PROTECTED1@@</q></p>

<p>or</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ implies @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Implication</title>
<statement>
<p>The implication</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is false only when <m>P</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is false.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Here <m>P</m> is called the <em>hypothesis</em> or <em>antecedent</em>, and <m>Q</m>
is called the <em>conclusion</em> or <em>consequent</em>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-implication-truth-table">
<title>Truth Table for Implication</title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
P &amp; Q &amp; P\to Q\\ \hline
T &amp; T &amp; T\\
T &amp; F &amp; F\\
F &amp; T &amp; T\\
F &amp; F &amp; T
\end{array}</md>
</p>

<p>The only false case is</p>

<p>
<md>T\to F.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-false-hypothesis-implication">
<title>Why a False Hypothesis Gives a True Implication</title>
<p>The truth table for implication sometimes feels unintuitive at first.</p>

<p>Consider the statement</p>

<p><q>If an integer is divisible by @@PROTECTED0@@, then it is even.</q></p>

<p>An integer such as <m>6</m> is not divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>The statement does not claim anything about what must happen when the integer
is not divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>6</m> does not provide a counterexample.</p>

<p>To make an implication false, one must find a case where the hypothesis is
true and the conclusion is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-necessary-sufficient">
<title>Necessary and Sufficient Conditions</title>
<p>The implication</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>can be expressed in several equivalent ways:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>if <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>P</m> implies <m>Q</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>P</m> is sufficient for <m>Q</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>Q</m> is necessary for <m>P</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>P</m> only if <m>Q</m>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The distinction between <q>if</q> and <q>only if</q> is especially important.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-only-if">
<title>Understanding <q>Only If</q></title>
<p>The statement</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ only if @@PROTECTED1@@</q></p>

<p>means</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus <m>Q</m> is required whenever <m>P</m> occurs.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p><q>An integer is divisible by @@PROTECTED0@@ only if it is even</q></p>

<p>means</p>

<p>
<md>4\mid n\to 2\mid n.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-converse">
<title>Converse</title>
<p>Given</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,</md>
</p>

<p>the <em>converse</em> is</p>

<p>
<md>Q\to P.</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>Original: If a number is divisible by <m>4</m>, then it is even.</p>

<p>The converse is</p>

<p>If a number is even, then it is divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>The original statement is true, but its converse is false.</p>

<p>For instance, <m>6</m> is even but not divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-inverse">
<title>Inverse</title>
<p>The <em>inverse</em> of</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P\to\neg Q.</md>
</p>

<p>For the previous example:</p>

<p>If a number is not divisible by <m>4</m>, then it is not even.</p>

<p>This statement is false because <m>6</m> is not divisible by <m>4</m> but is even.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-contrapositive">
<title>Contrapositive</title>
<p>The <em>contrapositive</em> of</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is</p>

<p>
<md>\neg Q\to\neg P.</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>If an integer is not even, then it is not divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>This statement is logically equivalent to the original implication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-implication-contrapositive-equivalence">
<title>Implication and Contrapositive</title>
<p>A fundamental logical equivalence is</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q\equiv\neg Q\to\neg P.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\equiv</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>logical equivalence symbol</em> in this context and is read</p>

<p><q>is logically equivalent to.</q></p>

<p>This equivalence is the foundation of proof by contraposition.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-converse-inverse-equivalence">
<title>Converse and Inverse</title>
<p>The converse and inverse are logically equivalent to each other:</p>

<p>
<md>Q\to P\equiv\neg P\to\neg Q.</md>
</p>

<p>However, neither is generally equivalent to the original implication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-biconditional">
<title>Biconditional</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\leftrightarrow</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>biconditional symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>if and only if.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Biconditional</title>
<statement>
<p>The biconditional</p>

<p>
<md>P\leftrightarrow Q</md>
</p>

<p>is true when <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> have the same truth value.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>It means both directions hold:</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Q\to P.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-biconditional-truth-table">
<title>Truth Table for Biconditional</title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
P &amp; Q &amp; P\leftrightarrow Q\\ \hline
T &amp; T &amp; T\\
T &amp; F &amp; F\\
F &amp; T &amp; F\\
F &amp; F &amp; T
\end{array}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-iff-language">
<title>The Phrase <q>If and Only If</q></title>
<p>The phrase</p>

<p><q>if and only if</q></p>

<p>is often abbreviated</p>

<p><q>iff.</q></p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>P\leftrightarrow Q</md>
</p>

<p>may be read</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ iff @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>A biconditional establishes both necessity and sufficiency.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-compound-propositions">
<title>Compound Propositions</title>
<p>Logical connectives may be combined to create more complicated statements.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>(P\land Q)\to R.</md>
</p>

<p>Parentheses specify how the statement is grouped.</p>

<p>As in arithmetic, changing the grouping may change the meaning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-constructing-truth-tables">
<title>Constructing Truth Tables</title>
<p>A truth table for two independent propositions requires</p>

<p>
<md>2^2=4</md>
</p>

<p>rows.</p>

<p>For three propositions, it requires</p>

<p>
<md>2^3=8</md>
</p>

<p>rows.</p>

<p>More generally, <m>n</m> independent proposition variables require</p>

<p>
<md>2^n</md>
</p>

<p>possible combinations of truth values.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-compound-truth-table">
<title>Worked Example: Compound Truth Table</title>
<example>
<title>Construct a Truth Table for <m>(P\land Q)\to P</m></title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
P &amp; Q &amp; P\land Q &amp; (P\land Q)\to P\\ \hline
T &amp; T &amp; T &amp; T\\
T &amp; F &amp; F &amp; T\\
F &amp; T &amp; F &amp; T\\
F &amp; F &amp; F &amp; T
\end{array}</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>The statement is true for every possible truth assignment.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-tautologies">
<title>Tautologies</title>
<definition>
<title>Tautology</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>tautology</em> is a compound proposition that is true for every possible
assignment of truth values.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P\lor\neg P</md>
</p>

<p>is always true.</p>

<p>This is called the <em>law of excluded middle</em> in classical logic.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-contradictions">
<title>Contradictions</title>
<definition>
<title>Contradiction</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>contradiction</em> is a compound proposition that is false for every
possible assignment of truth values.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P\land\neg P</md>
</p>

<p>is always false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-contingencies">
<title>Contingencies</title>
<definition>
<title>Contingency</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>contingency</em> is a proposition that is true for some truth assignments
and false for others.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P\land Q</md>
</p>

<p>is a contingency.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-equivalence">
<title>Logical Equivalence</title>
<p>Two propositions are logically equivalent if they have the same truth value
under every possible truth assignment.</p>

<p>We write</p>

<p>
<md>P\equiv Q.</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q\equiv\neg P\lor Q.</md>
</p>

<p>This identity is useful because an implication can be rewritten using only
negation and disjunction.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-double-negation">
<title>Double Negation</title>
<p>A proposition and its double negation are logically equivalent:</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(\neg P)\equiv P.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus <q>not not @@PROTECTED0@@</q> has the same truth value as <m>P</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-de-morgan-logic">
<title>De Morgan's Laws for Logic</title>
<p>Two fundamental logical identities are</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\land Q)\equiv\neg P\lor\neg Q,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\lor Q)\equiv\neg P\land\neg Q.</md>
</p>

<p>These are the logical versions of the set identities introduced in
<xref ref="subsec-set-de-morgan-laws" />.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-negating-statements">
<title>Negating Everyday Mathematical Statements</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ and @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>Its negation is</p>

<p><q>@@PROTECTED0@@ or @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>Symbolically,</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(x&gt;2\land x&lt;7)\equiv x\leq2\lor x\geq7.</md>
</p>

<p>This follows from De Morgan's laws.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-predicates">
<title>Predicates</title>
<definition>
<title>Predicate</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>predicate</em> is a statement containing one or more variables whose
truth value depends on the values assigned to those variables.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P(x):x^2&gt;4</md>
</p>

<p>is a predicate on <m>\R</m>.</p>

<p>It is true for some values of <m>x</m> and false for others.</p>

<p>For instance,</p>

<p>
<md>P(3)</md>
</p>

<p>is true, while</p>

<p>
<md>P(1)</md>
</p>

<p>is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-universal-quantifier">
<title>Universal Quantifier</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\forall</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>universal quantifier</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>for all</q></p>

<p>or</p>

<p><q>for every.</q></p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;x^2\geq0</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>For every real number @@PROTECTED0@@, @@PROTECTED1@@ is greater than or equal to zero.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-existential-quantifier">
<title>Existential Quantifier</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\exists</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>existential quantifier</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>there exists.</q></p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x\in\Z\text{ such that }x^2=9</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>There exists an integer @@PROTECTED0@@ such that @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>This statement is true because <m>x=3</m> and <m>x=-3</m> both work.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-unique-existence">
<title>Unique Existence</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\exists!</md>
</p>

<p>is read</p>

<p><q>there exists exactly one.</q></p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\exists!x\in\R\text{ such that }x+4=7.</md>
</p>

<p>This statement is true because the unique solution is</p>

<p>
<md>x=3.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-quantifier-domains">
<title>Quantifier Domains Matter</title>
<p>The truth of a quantified statement may depend on its domain.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x\in\R\text{ such that }x^2=2</md>
</p>

<p>is true.</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x\in\Q\text{ such that }x^2=2</md>
</p>

<p>is false.</p>

<p>Thus the universe over which a variable ranges is part of the statement.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-negating-universal">
<title>Negating Universal Statements</title>
<p>A universal statement is negated by changing</p>

<p>
<md>\forall</md>
</p>

<p>to</p>

<p>
<md>\exists</md>
</p>

<p>and negating the predicate.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(\forall x,\;P(x))\equiv\exists x,\;\neg P(x).</md>
</p>

<p>In words:</p>

<p><q>It is not true that every @@PROTECTED0@@ satisfies @@PROTECTED1@@</q></p>

<p>means</p>

<p><q>There exists at least one @@PROTECTED0@@ that does not satisfy @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-negating-existential">
<title>Negating Existential Statements</title>
<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(\exists x,\;P(x))\equiv\forall x,\;\neg P(x).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p><q>There does not exist an @@PROTECTED0@@ satisfying @@PROTECTED1@@</q></p>

<p>means</p>

<p><q>Every @@PROTECTED0@@ fails to satisfy @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-worked-negating-quantifier">
<title>Worked Example: Negating a Quantified Statement</title>
<example>
<title>Negate a Universal Statement</title>
<p>Negate</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;x^2+x+1&gt;0.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\exists x\in\R\text{ such that }x^2+x+1\leq0</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-multiple-quantifiers">
<title>Multiple Quantifiers</title>
<p>Statements may contain several quantifiers.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;\exists y\in\R\text{ such that }y&gt;x.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read</p>

<p><q>For every real number @@PROTECTED0@@, there exists a real number @@PROTECTED1@@ such that
@@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The statement is true.</p>

<p>For any <m>x</m>, one may choose</p>

<p>
<md>y=x+1.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-quantifier-order">
<title>Order of Quantifiers Matters</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;\exists y\in\R\text{ such that }y&gt;x.</md>
</p>

<p>This is true.</p>

<p>Now reverse the quantifiers:</p>

<p>
<md>\exists y\in\R,\;\forall x\in\R,\;y&gt;x.</md>
</p>

<p>This would say that there is one real number larger than every real number.</p>

<p>That statement is false.</p>

<p>Therefore the order of quantifiers can fundamentally change meaning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-translating-quantified-statements">
<title>Translating Quantified Statements</title>
<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in A,\;P(x)</md>
</p>

<p>means that every element of <m>A</m> satisfies <m>P</m>.</p>

<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x\in A,\;P(x)</md>
</p>

<p>means that at least one element of <m>A</m> satisfies <m>P</m>.</p>

<p>The statement</p>

<p>
<md>\exists!x\in A,\;P(x)</md>
</p>

<p>means exactly one element of <m>A</m> satisfies <m>P</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-arguments">
<title>Arguments</title>
<p>An <em>argument</em> consists of propositions called premises together with a
conclusion.</p>

<p>A valid argument is one in which the conclusion must be true whenever all
premises are true.</p>

<p>Validity concerns logical form.</p>

<p>It does not require the premises themselves to be true in the real world.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-therefore-symbol">
<title>Therefore Symbol</title>
<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\therefore</md>
</p>

<p>is called the <em>therefore symbol</em> and is read</p>

<p><q>therefore.</q></p>

<p>It is sometimes used to introduce the conclusion of an argument.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>P,\qquad P\to Q,\qquad \therefore Q.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-modus-ponens">
<title>Modus Ponens</title>
<definition>
<title>Modus Ponens</title>
<statement>
<p>The argument form</p>

<p>
<md>P,\qquad P\to Q,\qquad \therefore Q</md>
</p>

<p>is called <em>modus ponens</em>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>It may be read:</p>

<p><m>P</m> is true. If <m>P</m>, then <m>Q</m>. Therefore <m>Q</m>.</p>

<p>This is one of the most basic valid forms of mathematical reasoning.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-modus-tollens">
<title>Modus Tollens</title>
<definition>
<title>Modus Tollens</title>
<statement>
<p>The argument form</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad \neg Q,\qquad \therefore\neg P</md>
</p>

<p>is called <em>modus tollens</em>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>It relies on the equivalence between an implication and its contrapositive.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-hypothetical-syllogism">
<title>Hypothetical Syllogism</title>
<p>Another valid argument form is</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad Q\to R,\qquad \therefore P\to R.</md>
</p>

<p>This is called a <em>hypothetical syllogism</em>.</p>

<p>It expresses transitivity of implication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-disjunctive-syllogism">
<title>Disjunctive Syllogism</title>
<p>The argument</p>

<p>
<md>P\lor Q,\qquad \neg P,\qquad \therefore Q</md>
</p>

<p>is called a <em>disjunctive syllogism</em>.</p>

<p>If at least one of <m>P</m> or <m>Q</m> is true and <m>P</m> is false, then <m>Q</m>
must be true.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-affirming-consequent">
<title>Invalid Form: Affirming the Consequent</title>
<p>The argument form</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad Q,\qquad \therefore P</md>
</p>

<p>is invalid.</p>

<p>This error is called <em>affirming the consequent</em>.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>If a number is divisible by <m>4</m>, then it is even.</p>

<p><m>6</m> is even.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>6</m> is divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>The conclusion is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-denying-antecedent">
<title>Invalid Form: Denying the Antecedent</title>
<p>The argument form</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad \neg P,\qquad \therefore\neg Q</md>
</p>

<p>is also invalid.</p>

<p>This is called <em>denying the antecedent</em>.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>If an integer is divisible by <m>4</m>, then it is even.</p>

<p><m>6</m> is not divisible by <m>4</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>6</m> is not even.</p>

<p>Again, the conclusion is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-counterexamples">
<title>Counterexamples</title>
<p>A universal statement has the form</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in A,\;P(x).</md>
</p>

<p>To show that it is false, it is enough to find one element <m>a\in A</m> such
that</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P(a).</md>
</p>

<p>Such an example is called a <em>counterexample</em>.</p>

<p>For instance, the statement</p>

<p><q>Every prime number is odd</q></p>

<p>is false because</p>

<p>
<md>2</md>
</p>

<p>is prime and even.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-and-sets">
<title>Logic and Sets</title>
<p>Logical operations closely parallel set operations.</p>

<p>If <m>P(x)</m> and <m>Q(x)</m> are predicates, define</p>

<p>
<md>A=\{x:P(x)\},\qquad B=\{x:Q(x)\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>P(x)\lor Q(x)</md>
</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>A\cup B.</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>P(x)\land Q(x)</md>
</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>A\cap B.</md>
</p>

<p>Negation corresponds to set complement.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-set-de-morgan">
<title>Logic and De Morgan's Laws</title>
<p>The logical identity</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\lor Q)\equiv\neg P\land\neg Q</md>
</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>(A\cup B)^c=A^c\cap B^c.</md>
</p>

<p>Likewise,</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\land Q)\equiv\neg P\lor\neg Q</md>
</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>(A\cap B)^c=A^c\cup B^c.</md>
</p>

<p>This connection is one reason logical reasoning is so useful in set proofs.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-and-proof">
<title>Logic and Proof</title>
<p>Different logical forms suggest different proof strategies.</p>

<p>To prove</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,</md>
</p>

<p>one may use a direct proof or prove the contrapositive.</p>

<p>To disprove</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x,\;P(x),</md>
</p>

<p>one may provide a counterexample.</p>

<p>To prove</p>

<p>
<md>P\leftrightarrow Q,</md>
</p>

<p>one usually proves both</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Q\to P.</md>
</p>

<p>These methods are developed systematically in
<xref ref="sec-methods-of-proof" />.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-common-logic-errors">
<title>Common Logic Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-the-converse-is-automatically-true">
<title>Assuming the Converse Is Automatically True</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>one may not conclude</p>

<p>
<md>Q\to P.</md>
</p>

<p>The converse requires a separate argument.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-error-necessary-sufficient">
<title>Confusing Necessary and Sufficient</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,</md>
</p>

<p>then <m>P</m> is sufficient for <m>Q</m>, while <m>Q</m> is necessary for <m>P</m>.</p>

<p>Reversing these statements changes the logical meaning.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-error-partial-negation">
<title>Negating Only Part of a Statement</title>
<p>The negation of</p>

<p>
<md>P\land Q</md>
</p>

<p>is not</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P\land\neg Q.</md>
</p>

<p>The correct negation is</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P\lor\neg Q.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-error-negating-quantifier">
<title>Forgetting to Change the Quantifier</title>
<p>The negation of</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x,\;P(x)</md>
</p>

<p>is</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x,\;\neg P(x),</md>
</p>

<p>not</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x,\;\neg P(x).</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-error-quantifier-order">
<title>Ignoring Quantifier Order</title>
<p>The statements</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\exists y\,P(x,y)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\exists y\forall x\,P(x,y)</md>
</p>

<p>usually have different meanings.</p>

<p>Their quantifiers cannot generally be exchanged.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-error-evidence-proof">
<title>Treating Evidence as Proof</title>
<p>Checking many examples can suggest that a universal claim is true, but examples
alone do not prove it.</p>

<p>A proof must establish that the claim holds in every required case.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-worked-examples">
<title>Worked Examples</title>
<example>
<title>Write a Contrapositive</title>
<p>Consider the statement:</p>

<p>If <m>n^2</m> is odd, then <m>n</m> is odd.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>If <m>n</m> is even, then <m>n^2</m> is even.</p>
</example>

<example>
<title>Negate a Statement with Two Quantifiers</title>
<p>Negate</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;\exists y\in\R\text{ such that }y&gt;x.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\exists x\in\R,\;\forall y\in\R,\;y\leq x</md>
</p>
</example>

<example>
<title>Identify an Invalid Argument</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad Q,\qquad \therefore P.</md>
</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>The argument is invalid.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether each statement is a proposition:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><m>7+4=11</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>x+3=8</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><q>Close the door.</q>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>5&gt;9</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>If <m>P</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is false, determine the truth value of:</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P,\qquad P\land Q,\qquad P\lor Q,\qquad P\to Q,\qquad Q\to P.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct the truth table for</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\land Q).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct the truth table for</p>

<p>
<md>\neg P\lor Q.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use a truth table to verify</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q\equiv\neg P\lor Q.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use a truth table to verify</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q\equiv\neg Q\to\neg P.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write the converse, inverse, and contrapositive of:</p>

<p>If an integer is divisible by <m>6</m>, then it is divisible by <m>3</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Translate</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>using the words <q>sufficient</q> and <q>necessary.</q></p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>P\lor\neg P</md>
</p>

<p>is a tautology, contradiction, or contingency.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>P\land\neg P</md>
</p>

<p>is a tautology, contradiction, or contingency.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Negate:</p>

<p>
<md>x&gt;2\text{ and }x&lt;8.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Negate:</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq-1\text{ or }x\geq5.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write in words:</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;x^2\geq0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write symbolically:</p>

<p>There exists an integer whose square equals <m>16</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Negate:</p>

<p>
<md>\forall n\in\Z,\;n^2\geq0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Negate:</p>

<p>
<md>\exists x\in\R\text{ such that }x^2+1=0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;\exists y\in\R,\;y&gt;x</md>
</p>

<p>is true.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why</p>

<p>
<md>\exists y\in\R,\;\forall x\in\R,\;y&gt;x</md>
</p>

<p>is false.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Identify the argument form:</p>

<p>
<md>P,\qquad P\to Q,\qquad \therefore Q.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Identify the error in:</p>

<p>
<md>P\to Q,\qquad Q,\qquad \therefore P.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Give a counterexample to:</p>

<p>Every positive integer is prime.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove using a truth table that</p>

<p>
<md>\neg(P\lor Q)\equiv\neg P\land\neg Q.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove using a truth table that</p>

<p>
<md>P\leftrightarrow Q\equiv(P\to Q)\land(Q\to P).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Negate the statement</p>

<p>
<md>\forall x\in\R,\;\exists y\in\R,\;\forall z\in\R,\;P(x,y,z).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-logic-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Logic supplies the formal structure behind the proof methods developed in
<xref ref="sec-methods-of-proof" />. Logical conjunction, disjunction, and negation
parallel intersection, union, and complement from
<xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory" />. Quantifiers occur throughout analysis,
algebra, topology, probability, and nearly every later chapter whenever
mathematical statements describe all objects or assert the existence of
particular objects.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
